# redox-os/base @ 8f576d27: Pinebook Pro initfs daemons and platform fixes
#
# Target repository : https://gitlab.redox-os.org/redox-os/base.git
# Base commit       : 8f576d27ee5d028157b001e320affc7b1ec2736b (main, 2026-11)
# Reason            : Pinebook Pro (RK3399) bring-up needs four initfs-only
#                     additions plus one display fix that upstream does not have.
#
#                     1. Interim serial console (experiments 4-5): the boot
#                        stops at the initfs phase until storage works, so
#                        there is no root filesystem, shell or login.
#                        `debugsh` is a small static initfs daemon that opens
#                        the kernel `debug` scheme (/scheme/debug), gives the
#                        UART a line discipline (echo, backspace) plus
#                        built-ins (ls/cat/echo>/hexdump/env/exec/reboot), sets its
#                        working directory to /scheme/initfs so a bare `ls`
#                        works, and maps `ls /` to the scheme namespace. It
#                        waits up to 3 s for a `disk.mmc` scheme at startup
#                        (and rechecks on each keypress) and exits when it
#                        appears: storage works, so the real login owns the
#                        serial console instead of the initfs shell.
#                        `hwd` no longer spawns `pcid` on aarch64 (hwd spawns
#                        it directly, bypassing init services, and pcid's
#                        PCI 3.0 configuration-space fallback is `todo!()`
#                        on aarch64); both pcid-spawner services are gated to
#                        x86/x86_64 for the same reason.
#
#                     2. microSD storage (experiment 6): `dwmmcd`, a static
#                        initfs driver for the RK3399 DW MMC (dw_mshc)
#                        microSD controller at fe320000, found by compatible
#                        string plus reg address (the SDIO controller shares
#                        the compatible string; the eMMC at fe330000 holds
#                        the baseline OS and must never be probed). FIFO/PIO
#                        mode only, polled, no IDMAC and no controller
#                        interrupts (U-Boot fifo_mode model). Clock strategy
#                        (iteration 6, U-Boot rk3399 model): controller
#                        CLKDIV stays 0 (bypass, internal /2) and the card
#                        rate comes from CRU CLKSEL_CON16 (xin24m/30 = 400
#                        kHz identification, the U-Boot-inherited value for
#                        data); CLKENA changes are wrapped in two CIU
#                        clock-update commands, each awaited by polling the
#                        CMD START bit exactly like dwmci_setup_bus (U-Boot
#                        v2021.07 never waits for CDONE on clock-only
#                        commands; neither does this IP raise it). CLKSEL_CON16
#                        follows the Rockchip write-mask convention (mux
#                        [10:8] + div [6:0], mask in [31:16]; plain writes
#                        are dropped by the hardware), so every rate write
#                        is hiword-masked and read back. If an update never
#                        completes, the driver falls back to plain register
#                        writes with no CIU involvement, and clock failures
#                        degrade to a slower bus instead of aborting. Delays
#                        are busy-waits: the bring-up kernel never wakes
#                        nanosleep on the generic timer. All
#                        timeouts use the kernel monotonic clock so a dead
#                        card cannot hang the boot. Register map follows
#                        Linux drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.h and U-Boot v2021.07
#                        include/dwmmc.h (the U-Boot version installed on
#                        the Pinebook Pro). Bring-up logging is mirrored to
#                        /scheme/debug so it is visible on the serial
#                        console. Serves the `disk.mmc` scheme (the name
#                        redoxfs scans for), ordered before 50_rootfs.service
#                        via 40_drivers.target: init blocks on notify-type
#                        services, so redoxfs only starts after the scheme is
#                        registered.
#
#                     3. Display colour (experiment 7): UEFI GOP does not
#                        guarantee a 32-bit linear framebuffer. U-Boot v2021.07
#                        programs the Rockchip VOP in RGB565 for the eDP panel
#                        (rk_vop.c forces VIDEO_BPP16 for VOP_MODE_EDP) and
#                        reports PixelBitMask 0xf800/0x07e0/0x001f, so 32-bit
#                        ARGB writes turned the VT background into a
#                        black/yellow dither. `vesad` now reads
#                        FRAMEBUFFER_BPP (default 32 when absent, so x86 is
#                        unchanged), maps the real framebuffer size and
#                        converts client ARGB8888 buffers to RGB565 while
#                        copying them to the framebuffer; the clear path uses
#                        the same pixel size. `FRAMEBUFFER{n}` gained an
#                        optional fifth field (bpp) for additional outputs.
#
#                     4. USB input (experiment 8): `ohcid`, a static initfs
#                        OHCI host controller driver for the RK3399 USB2
#                        ports. U-Boot only brings up the OHCI halves of the
#                        two EHCI/OHCI pairs, and the internal hub with the
#                        keyboard and touchpad sits there. The driver maps
#                        the controller through physmap, allocates
#                        physically-contiguous descriptors through
#                        common::dma, resets the root port(s), enumerates one
#                        level of hub (class requests, port power/reset),
#                        reads the configuration descriptors, switches a boot
#                        keyboard to the boot protocol, and polls its
#                        interrupt endpoint from one periodic slot (~32 ms).
#                        Translated HID usages become `inputd` events, the
#                        same path usbhidd uses. Everything is polled: no
#                        controller interrupts, no nanosleep (the kernel does
#                        not wake sleepers on the generic timer).
#
# Contents:
#   * debugsh/                  new initfs-only crate (static binary;
#                               `reboot` writes `reset` to the kernel sys:kstop
#                               scheme -> PSCI SYSTEM_RESET)
#   * drivers/storage/dwmmcd/   new initfs-only crate (static binary)
#   * drivers/graphics/vesad/src/main.rs, scheme.rs
#                               honour FRAMEBUFFER_BPP (RGB565)
#   * drivers/usb/ohcid/        new initfs-only crate (static binary)
#   * init.initfs.d/40_ohcid.service            USB input (aarch64 only)
#   * Makefile                  add debugsh, dwmmcd and ohcid to INITFS_BINS
#   * Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock    workspace members + lock entries
#   * init.initfs.d/95_debugsh.service          debug console (aarch64 only)
#   * init.initfs.d/40_dwmmcd.service           microSD driver (aarch64 only)
#   * init.initfs.d/40_drivers.target            start dwmmcd with the drivers
#   * init.initfs.d/90_initfs.target             start debugsh with the target
#   * drivers/hwd/src/main.rs   do not spawn `pcid` from hwd on aarch64
#   * init.initfs.d/40_pcid-spawner-initfs.service, init.d/00_pcid-spawner.service:
#     gate pcid-spawner to x86/x86_64
#
# Apply from recipes/core/base/source:  git apply <this file>
